{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 9 and 10 distinguish exact scoring, repeatability and validity for the stated grid comparison. One run documents performance; repeated-run precision is unestablished, corresponding to rating 0 rather than demonstrated inadequacy.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "The execution history needed to establish repeated testing is missing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, explicitly conducting one run contradicts repeated testing but leaves repeatability unknown. Exact scoring and a narrow intended use do not supply precision evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once",
          "reason": "Without repeats, neither stability nor instability is established."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 supports assessing repeated-run precision using the reported range, without treating it as a reliability coefficient. The results meet the local stability rule; §10 limits interpretation to the stated grid comparison.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations held the specified conditions constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The two-percentage-point range meets the stated maximum of two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 separates the existence of a repeatability study from its outcome. Repeats were conducted, but their range fails the stated stability requirement; this is adverse evidence rather than missing evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly reports repeats under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The 70-percentage-point range exceeds the stated maximum of two."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 recognises installation guidance as procedural documentation. It does not replace §9 precision evidence, which remains insufficient; the benchmark's actual repeatability is unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation instructions do not establish an execution history."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added documentation supplies no repeated-run findings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 evaluates interpretations against intended use. The observed counts support this bounded grid comparison; they do not establish general intelligence. Norms may be unnecessary for the explicitly limited raw-performance comparison under §8.3.2.2.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A solved more evaluated puzzles under the reported conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not justify an inference across domains."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6 and 10 require evidence appropriate to the claimed construct and use. Grid performance supports the narrow comparison, but the stated general-intelligence selection purpose lacks cross-domain validity evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "The observed counts establish better performance by A on these puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Stating a broad intended use does not validate that interpretation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2 recognise experimental and relevant-outcome evidence. Random allocation, blinded correctness checking and the reported interval support workshop-specific correctness benefits. They do not establish unmeasured safety benefits or broader generalisation.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Initial scores alone provide no demonstrated link to improved workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomised trial and positive difference interval support better correctness in the studied conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Decision correctness findings do not establish an effect on unmeasured safety outcomes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6 distinguishes task and scoring documentation from §9 precision evidence. A headline score without execution history establishes neither repeated testing nor instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The passage does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One score cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 treats the single confirmed evaluation as insufficient precision evidence. Absence of repeats contradicts the study claim but does not demonstrate unstable scores.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The execution record explicitly rules out repeated testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "A single evaluation provides no observed between-run instability."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 permits judging the reported repeated-run precision in context. The two-point range satisfies the report's three-point rule; this does not establish all forms of reliability or validity.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems were evaluated repeatedly under the specified matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-point range is within the three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 distinguishes adequate reporting of repeat evaluations from satisfactory precision. The observed range fails the benchmark's stated stability rule, without needing a psychometric coefficient conversion.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1, 6.3.2 and 9 distinguish content development, scoring instructions and precision evidence. Topic and marking descriptions establish neither answer errors nor repeated-run testing.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Repeated identical-condition evaluations are not documented."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No answer errors are demonstrated; their absence is also unestablished."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 credits the appendix as technical guidance, without transferring that evidence to §9 precision or §§6.1 and 7 content quality. Both substantive claims remain unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results or examination of answers.",
          "reason": "The appendix supplies no repeated-evaluation evidence."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "Neither the original document nor the appendix demonstrates answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.1.3 recognises independent editorial review as development evidence. It does not prove error-free answers. Missing pre-reconciliation agreement prevents an agreement assessment; editorial agreement is distinct from operational scoring reliability under §9.6.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The development review covered both questions and supplied answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Review activity alone does not establish that all released answers are correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1 and 7 distinguish development review from resulting content quality. Review occurred, but twelve released answer errors are explicit adverse evidence. Their impact on scores and interpretations requires examination.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both components underwent development review."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "identifies twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Explicit released-version errors contradict universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3 and 6.3.2 separate deterministic scoring from verified scoring correctness. No correctness comparison is reported. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable if the fixed rule involves no judgemental scoring.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "An unreported comparison cannot be assumed to have occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marking does not establish correct marking for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 treats the correctness comparison as relevant scoring verification. Two unresolved discrepancies provide adverse evidence despite determinism; the 20 chosen responses do not establish universal accuracy.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The required comparison was explicitly conducted."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "The reported mismatches with independently established correct marks conflict with universal scoring correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 judges validity for the explicitly bounded use: identifying the higher observed count. The findings suffice for that use. Under §8.3.2.2, this raw-score comparison need not require norms.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer the stated descriptive question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A's observed count is higher."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6 and 10 require support for the stated general-intelligence interpretation. The grid counts establish only the local ordering; evidence sufficient for comparisons across subjects and real-world settings is absent.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The material does not establish sufficient validity for the broad intended use."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The local performance comparison remains supported regardless of the broader intended use."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 6.3.2 and 10 assess scoring against its declared interpretation. Exact-grid scoring matches the complete-output requirement; excluding partial credit is not a demonstrated defect for this purpose.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The stated scoring requirement is to count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "The one-or-zero rule implements the stated requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial grids follows the requirement rather than violating it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3, 6.3.2 and 10 identify an explicit mismatch between scoring and intended interpretation. Giving zero for partial grids fails the declared cell-level credit requirement.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "A partially correct grid receives zero.",
          "reason": "Zero partial credit conflicts with the requirement to credit every correctly completed cell."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell, including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The stated requirement explicitly includes credit that the rule withholds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.8, 6.2.4 and 9 distinguish administration changes from identical-condition precision. This study examines instruction variation; it does not establish repeatability with fixed instructions or equivalence between instruction versions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions differ, so the evaluations do not have identical conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the other listed conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 recognises the design as studying identical-condition score variation. Without numerical findings, its precision outcome cannot be judged. EFPA §9.4 equivalence across instruction versions is not tested.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "The software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported score changes were studied under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes instruction changes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "Applied the supplied EFPA Test Review Model 2025, version 5.0, Parts A–C, using case-focused explanations as permitted. Attribution: European Federation of Psychologists’ Associations AISBL (2025), https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. Reinterpretations: test taker means evaluated AI; administration means execution conditions; repeatability means consistency of fixed-system scores; validity concerns the stated score interpretation and use. A local stability rule is not an EFPA reliability coefficient. Human age norms, sensory or motor prerequisites, clinical diagnosis, professional purchasing qualifications and personal debriefing are inapplicable literally; language and interface demands, operator competence and stakeholder reporting may have AI-evaluation analogues. All original domains were considered: factual description, classification, scoring, reports, supply, rationale and documentation, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair use and final evaluation. Unprovided evidence on relevant domains remains unknown, not inadequate; conditional methods and digital-report reviews cannot be presumed applicable. Section 12 would require at least three scored report profiles if applicable. No section ratings, aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied. If formal ratings were assigned, 0 would mean insufficient evidence and 1 demonstrated inadequacy; critical 0/1 ratings would invoke EFPA's restricted-use recommendation. Bibliographic basis is solely the supplied model and constructed passages; no external sources were consulted. No instructions required clarification. The extraction's inconsistent section-7 summary numbering and malformed validity anchors do not affect these claim classifications."
}
